1
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,700
Hey, Leonard, check this out.

2
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:12,230
Leonard, she's doing it again.

3
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,860
I think it upsets Sheldon when you play with the food.

4
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:19,300
No. It upsets Sheldon when she willy-nilly takes it

5
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:20,830
from the containers without regard

6
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,200
for its equitable distribution.

7
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:26,230
This is essentially why you have famine in India.

8
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:30,960
You want me to put it back?

9
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:32,560
Leonard.

10
00:00:32,630 --> 00:00:36,500
It upsets Sheldon when you play with the Sheldon.

11
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,930
What's up, my nerdizzles?

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:42,260
Raj, Sheldon,

13
00:00:42,330 --> 00:00:44,200
I want you to meet my girlfriend Bernadette.

14
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:45,300
Hello. Leonard, Penny,

15
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:46,960
you know my girlfriend Bernadette.

16
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:48,360
- Yeah.  - Hey.

17
00:00:48,430 --> 00:00:50,630
Bernadette, say fo'shizzle to my nerdizzles.

18
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:54,160
I don't think I can.

19
00:00:54,230 --> 00:00:56,860
I don't have Howard's street cred.

20
00:00:57,960 --> 00:00:58,960
I hope it's all right--

21
00:00:59,030 --> 00:01:00,300
I told my girlfriend Bernadette

22
00:01:00,360 --> 00:01:01,830
she could join us for dinner.

23
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:02,830
Sure. The more, the merrier.

24
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:04,960
Wa-- no, that's a false equivalency.

25
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:06,500
More does not equal merry.

26
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:08,960
If there were 2,000 people in this apartment right now,

27
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:11,560
would we be celebrating? No, we'd be suffocating.

28
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:13,760
- Sheldon...  - Don't "Sheldon" me.

29
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:15,100
We ordered for five people, not six.

30
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:16,630
Oh, come on, it's fine.

31
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,060
We'll just put it all on the table, you know, family style.

32
00:01:19,130 --> 00:01:20,430
Oh, sure. And while we're at it,

33
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:22,200
why don't we put our hands behind our backs,

34
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:23,660
have an old-fashioned eating contest?

35
00:01:23,730 --> 00:01:25,060
Relax, it'll be fine.

36
00:01:25,130 --> 00:01:26,130
Sit down, you guys.

37
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:29,600
No! No! No!

38
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:30,500
What?!

39
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:34,260
Oh, yeah, you can't sit there.

40
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:35,200
Why not?

41
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:36,760
That's where Sheldon sits.

42
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:38,300
He can't sit somewhere else?

43
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:41,460
No, no, no-- you see, in the winter,

44
00:01:41,530 --> 00:01:43,830
that seat is close enough to the radiator so that he's warm,

45
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,260
yet not so close that he sweats.

46
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:47,260
In the summer, it's directly in the path of

47
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:49,630
a cross-breeze created by opening windows there and there.

48
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,960
It faces the television at an angle that isn't direct,

49
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:53,430
so he can still talk to everybody,

50
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,200
yet not so wide that the picture looks distorted.

51
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:02,500
Perhaps there's hope for you after all.

52
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,560
Ooh, I love your shoes.

53
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:32,560
Oh, thanks.

54
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:33,760
They are cute, aren't they?

55
00:02:33,830 --> 00:02:35,660
- Where'd you get them?  - Shoes for Less.

56
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:37,100
I've been meaning to go over there.

57
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,300
Oh, great selection, great prices.

58
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:43,330
My mother was right. Hell is real.

59
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:45,500
Come on, Sheldon.

60
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,360
Let the womenfolk chat.

61
00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:51,300
Womenfolk?

62
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:52,730
Gals?

63
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:53,860
Chicks?

64
00:02:53,930 --> 00:02:56,960
Utero-Americans?

65
00:02:57,030 --> 00:02:59,230
Just eat your dinner.

66
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,960
Don't take him too seriously.

67
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:04,730
A lot of what he says is intended as humor.

68
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,000
Yeah, well, I don't think it's very funny.

69
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:10,660
Me neither, but he just lights up when I laugh.

70
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,600
Howard, never let her go.

71
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:19,460
So, Leonard, Howard says you're working on

72
00:03:19,530 --> 00:03:21,160
fundamental tests of quantum mechanics.

73
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:22,200
I am.

74
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:23,730
Are you interested in physics?

75
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,100
Oh, I find it fascinating.

76
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,530
If I hadn't gone into microbiology,

77
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:29,260
I probably would have gone into physics.

78
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:32,200
Or ice dancing.

79
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:34,360
Actually, my tests of the Aharonov-Bohm

80
00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:36,860
quantum interference effect have reached an interesting point.

81
00:03:36,930 --> 00:03:38,360
Right now, we're testing the phase shift

82
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:39,760
due to an electric potential.

83
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:40,900
That's amazing.

84
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:44,000
Yes. Leonard's work is nearly as amazing

85
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:47,230
as third graders growing lima beans in wet paper towels.

86
00:03:49,230 --> 00:03:51,660
While I appreciate the "Oh, snap,"

87
00:03:51,730 --> 00:03:54,400
I'm uncomfortable having your moist breath in my ear.

88
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,530
Are you going to try

89
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,800
to set up the voltages using tunnel junctions?

90
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:03,900
Yes, I am.

91
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:05,930
You want to see a simulation on my laptop?

92
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,460
Oh, yeah, show me.

93
00:04:07,530 --> 00:04:09,830
In microbiology, the most exciting thing

94
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:12,060
I get to work with is yeast.

95
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:13,900
Howard?

96
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:14,930
Yeah?

97
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,630
Your shoes are delightful.

98
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:22,360
Where did you get them?

99
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:23,260
What?

100
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:25,600
Bazinga. I don't care.

101
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:35,100
Ha! Eat my dust,

102
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,160
racially stereotypical plumber.

103
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,830
That's not fair.

104
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,560
I got stuck behind a tree.

105
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:45,460
And a cow and a penguin.

106
00:04:45,530 --> 00:04:47,400
Face it, dude,

107
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:49,900
whether it's a real car or a virtual cartoon car,

108
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:52,200
you can't drive.

109
00:04:52,260 --> 00:04:53,960
Just need a little more practice.

110
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:57,430
What you need is cheat codes, motor skills and a magic genie

111
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:00,930
who grants wishes to little boys who suck at Mario Kart.

112
00:05:03,030 --> 00:05:06,000
Hey, Sheldon, can I talk to you for a second?

113
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:08,630
It's not about shoes, is it?

114
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,800
I don't think I could go through that again.

115
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:12,300
It's not about shoes.

116
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:13,560
Then speak.

117
00:05:13,630 --> 00:05:16,300
Um, actually, can we do it in private?

118
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:17,130
All right.

119
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:19,660
Go away.

120
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,100
I agree, it's rude, but she asked for privacy.

121
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:26,230
Thanks, Raj.

122
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:29,030
Okay, so here's the thing:

123
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:32,660
I was wondering if you could maybe teach me a little physics?

124
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:35,400
A little physics?

125
00:05:36,730 --> 00:05:37,800
There's no such thing.

126
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,800
Physics encompasses the entire universe,

127
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,400
from quantum particles to supernovas,

128
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:45,660
from spinning electrons to spinning galaxies.

129
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:46,660
Yeah, okay, cool.

130
00:05:46,730 --> 00:05:47,900
I don't need the PBS special.

131
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,330
I just want to know enough

132
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,430
so I can talk to Leonard about his job.

133
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:52,860
You know, like Bernadette does.

134
00:05:52,930 --> 00:05:54,460
Why can't Leonard teach you?

135
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:56,000
'Cause I want to surprise him.

136
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:58,100
Can't you surprise him in some other way?

137
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,100
For example, I'm sure he'd be delightfully taken aback

138
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,330
if you cleaned your apartment.

139
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,000
Come on, Sheldon, this is important to me.

140
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:06,130
Penny,

141
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:07,530
this would be a massive undertaking,

142
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,360
and my time is both limited and valuable.

143
00:06:10,430 --> 00:06:12,900
You're sitting here playing video games all day.

144
00:06:13,930 --> 00:06:15,460
Okay, point.

145
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:18,800
What sort of foundation do you have?

146
00:06:18,860 --> 00:06:21,230
Did you take any science classes in school?

147
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:23,400
Sure. I did the one with the frogs.

148
00:06:26,430 --> 00:06:29,230
The one with the frogs.

149
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:30,630
Yeah, actually, it was pretty cool.

150
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:33,730
A lot of the girls threw up, but I gutted that thing like a deer.

151
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:35,330
I'm sorry, Penny.

152
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:36,700
I don't think so.

153
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:37,860
Oh, come on!

154
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:39,660
A smart guy like you, it'll be a challenge.

155
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:41,160
You can make it like an experiment.

156
00:06:43,230 --> 00:06:45,560
Interesting.

157
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:47,900
I suppose if someone could teach sign language

158
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,830
to KoKo the gorilla...

159
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:53,260
I could teach you some rudimentary physics.

160
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:54,260
Great!

161
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:56,500
It's a little insulting, but great.

162
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:57,500
I'll be KoKo.

163
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:58,700
Not likely.

164
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:00,630
KoKo learned to understand over 2,000 words,

165
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:04,330
not one of which had anything to do with shoes.

166
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:06,900
Hey, fellas.

167
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,030
This is my girlfriend Bernadette.

168
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,760
My girlfriend Bernadette.

169
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:13,230
Who are all those people?

170
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,400
Have no idea.

171
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:16,430
Hey, Leonard.

172
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:17,860
Hi. Hey, look,

173
00:07:17,930 --> 00:07:20,530
it's Howard and his girlfriend Bernadette.

174
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,030
Thought I'd give the little woman a tour of the old salt mines.

175
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:26,600
He doesn't mean salt mines.

176
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:29,260
He means where he works.

177
00:07:29,330 --> 00:07:31,760
Yeah, no, I got it.

178
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:35,530
So, how's your experiment going?

179
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:36,860
Ah, terrific.

180
00:07:36,930 --> 00:07:38,260
We're getting the electron accelerator set up.

181
00:07:38,330 --> 00:07:40,200
We should be ready to go day after tomorrow.

182
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:42,160
Boy, I'd love to see that.

183
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:43,300
You're welcome to come.

184
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,460
Really? Oh, that'd be great.

185
00:07:45,530 --> 00:07:47,260
How exciting is that?

186
00:07:47,330 --> 00:07:50,160
Like Hanukkah in July.

187
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:52,230
Do they have that?

188
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,730
No.

189
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:58,660
You got me again.

190
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:03,100
This isn't non-fat yogurt.

191
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:04,800
This is fatty fat fat.

192
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,130
Excuse me.

193
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:08,460
Could you grab me another napkin, sweetie?

194
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:09,460
- Sure. - Thanks, honey.

195
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:13,860
All right, what is your deal?

196
00:08:13,930 --> 00:08:15,230
Excuse me?

197
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:16,500
Inviting my girlfriend

198
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:18,960
to come see your electron accelerator?

199
00:08:19,030 --> 00:08:20,630
Yeah? So?

200
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:25,430
You really are a piece of work.

201
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:26,560
It's not enough

202
00:08:26,630 --> 00:08:28,430
you get the prom queen, you have to get

203
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:30,600
the head of the decorating committee, too?

204
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:32,700
What are you talking about?

205
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:34,460
Don't play innocent with me.

206
00:08:34,530 --> 00:08:35,930
I practically invented

207
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,430
using fancy lab equipment to seduce women.

208
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:41,200
Has it ever worked?

209
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:43,560
Not so far, but that's not the point!

210
00:08:43,630 --> 00:08:44,760
Howard, relax.

211
00:08:44,830 --> 00:08:47,500
I'm not interested in your girlfriend.

212
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:48,760
I hope not.

213
00:08:48,830 --> 00:08:51,830
Because you don't want to mess with me.

214
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:59,960
I'm crazy.

215
00:09:00,030 --> 00:09:03,060
I believe you.

216
00:09:04,430 --> 00:09:06,800
Research journal, entry one.

217
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:08,400
I'm about to embark on one of

218
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:12,730
the great challenges of my scientific career:

219
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:16,430
teaching Penny physics.

220
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:18,830
I'm calling it Project Gorilla.

221
00:09:20,830 --> 00:09:22,730
Hey, Sheldon.

222
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:24,400
Come in. Take a seat.

223
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:27,830
Subject has arrived.

224
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:31,160
I've extended a friendly casual greeting.

225
00:09:31,230 --> 00:09:32,760
Ready to get started?

226
00:09:32,830 --> 00:09:34,030
One moment.

227
00:09:38,530 --> 00:09:42,700
Subject appears well-rested and enthusiastic.

228
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:44,760
Apparently, ignorance is bliss.

229
00:09:47,330 --> 00:09:50,660
All right, let us begin.

230
00:09:50,730 --> 00:09:52,330
Where's your notebook?

231
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:54,400
Um, I don't have one.

232
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:56,900
How are you going to take notes without a notebook?

233
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:58,800
I have to take notes?

234
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:01,100
How else are you gonna study for the tests?

235
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:02,460
There's gonna be a test?

236
00:10:02,530 --> 00:10:04,960
Test-sss.

237
00:10:06,930 --> 00:10:08,430
Here.

238
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:09,560
It's college-ruled.

239
00:10:09,630 --> 00:10:11,300
I hope that's not too intimidating.

240
00:10:14,330 --> 00:10:15,360
Thank you.

241
00:10:15,430 --> 00:10:16,360
You're welcome.

242
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:18,260
Now, Introduction to Physics.

243
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:21,130
What is physics?

244
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:25,130
Physics comes from the ancient Greek word "physika."

245
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:29,530
It's at this point that you'll want to start taking notes.

246
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:34,860
"Physika" means the science of natural things.

247
00:10:34,930 --> 00:10:39,000
And it is there, in ancient Greece, that our story begins.

248
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:40,630
- Ancient Greece?!  - Hush.

249
00:10:40,700 --> 00:10:42,700
If you have questions, raise your hand.

250
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:47,460
It's a warm summer evening, circa 600 BC.

251
00:10:47,530 --> 00:10:51,530
You've finished your shopping at the local market, or Agora...

252
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:57,400
...And you look up at the night sky.

253
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:01,100
There you notice some of the stars seem to move,

254
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:05,730
So you name them "planetes," or "wanderer".

255
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:12,930
Yes, Penny?

256
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,160
Um, does this have anything to do with Leonard's work?

257
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:19,600
This is the beginning of a 2,600-year journey

258
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:21,560
We're going to take together

259
00:11:21,630 --> 00:11:22,830
From the ancient Greeks

260
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:26,400
Through Isaac Newton to Niels Bohr

261
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:28,100
To Erwin Schrodinger

262
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:29,700
To the Dutch researchers

263
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:31,760
That Leonard is currently ripping off.

264
00:11:31,830 --> 00:11:35,360
2,600 years?

265
00:11:35,430 --> 00:11:36,560
Yeah, give or take.

266
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:38,960
As I was saying, it's a warm summer evening

267
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:40,500
In ancient Greece...

268
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:42,360
Yes, Penny?

269
00:11:42,430 --> 00:11:44,200
I have to go to the bathroom.

270
00:11:44,260 --> 00:11:45,460
Can't you hold it?

271
00:11:45,530 --> 00:11:48,130
Not for 2,600 years.

272
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,130
Project Gorilla, entry two.

273
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:02,400
I am exhausted.

274
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:20,260
- Howard?  - Huh?

275
00:12:20,330 --> 00:12:23,000
It unhooks in the front.

276
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,530
Oh, that explains a lot.

277
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:33,360
Howard, I'm home!

278
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:35,370
Of course.

279
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:38,170
Senior fitness was cancelled.

280
00:12:38,240 --> 00:12:41,270
It turns out you can forget how to ride a bike.

281
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:47,070
I'm fine, but, oy, did Sam Harpootian eat gravel.

282
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:49,440
That's great, Ma!

283
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:52,200
What's great about an 80-year-old Armenian man

284
00:12:52,270 --> 00:12:54,940
With half his chin scraped off?!

285
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,500
I guess I should go.

286
00:12:56,570 --> 00:12:57,870
No, no, don't move.

287
00:12:57,940 --> 00:13:00,970
Hey, Ma, can I have lamb stew for dinner?

288
00:13:01,040 --> 00:13:04,440
Lamb stew? I'd have to go to the supermarket.

289
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:05,700
Please?

290
00:13:05,770 --> 00:13:07,740
I got a real hankering.

291
00:13:10,070 --> 00:13:14,800
Oh, I can't say no to my little tushy face.

292
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:16,940
I'll be back soon.

293
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,200
Thanks, Ma.

294
00:13:21,140 --> 00:13:24,270
Do you want the regular peas or the Le Seur?

295
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:27,600
Always Le Seur peas with lamb stew!

296
00:13:27,670 --> 00:13:30,570
You're right! When you're right, you're right!

297
00:13:32,970 --> 00:13:34,800
What if they're out of the Le Seur?

298
00:13:34,870 --> 00:13:36,870
Then get the regular!

299
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:40,240
All right! You don't have to yell!

300
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:43,970
Sorry about that.

301
00:13:45,570 --> 00:13:48,170
Let me just put that on vibrate.

302
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:49,670
I'm already on vibrate.

303
00:13:51,770 --> 00:13:53,470
You know, that one I got.

304
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:57,470
Howard, did you say something to Leonard about me?

305
00:13:57,540 --> 00:13:58,870
Uh, what do you mean?

306
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:01,000
He says if I go see his experiment tomorrow

307
00:14:01,070 --> 00:14:02,800
It might weird you out.

308
00:14:02,870 --> 00:14:04,540
Really? He said that?

309
00:14:04,600 --> 00:14:06,540
You're not jealous of Leonard, are you?

310
00:14:06,600 --> 00:14:08,670
Me? No.

311
00:14:08,740 --> 00:14:12,100
I may have mentioned that it's a little inappropriate

312
00:14:12,170 --> 00:14:14,900
To be asking another man's girlfriend to his experiment

313
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:17,570
Without first discussing it with said man.

314
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:20,000
Are you saying I need to ask your permission to hang out with Leonard?

315
00:14:20,070 --> 00:14:21,940
I didn't say anything like that.

316
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:26,340
I said Leonard has to ask my permission.

317
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:31,870
Come on, I don't want to eat lamb stew with my mother.

318
00:14:31,940 --> 00:14:34,970
Damn, I was this close on the bra.

319
00:14:37,640 --> 00:14:42,270
Now, remember, Newton realized that Aristotle was wrong

320
00:14:42,340 --> 00:14:44,640
And force was not necessary to maintain motion.

321
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:47,770
So let's plug in our 9.8 meters per second squared

322
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,700
As "A" and we get

323
00:14:49,770 --> 00:14:53,370
Force-- Earth gravity- equals mass times 9.8 meters

324
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:55,100
Per second per second.

325
00:14:55,170 --> 00:14:57,600
So we can see that "ma" equals "mg"

326
00:14:57,670 --> 00:14:59,340
And what do we know from this?

327
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,900
Uh, we know that...

328
00:15:06,970 --> 00:15:08,540
...Newton was a really smart cookie.

329
00:15:10,070 --> 00:15:13,070
Oh! Is that where Fig Newtons come from?

330
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:17,100
No, Fig Newtons are named after a small town in Massachusetts.

331
00:15:17,170 --> 00:15:20,770
- Don't write that down!  - Sorry.

332
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:24,340
Now, if "ma" equals "mg," what does that imply?

333
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:25,500
I don't know.

334
00:15:25,570 --> 00:15:27,070
How can you not know?

335
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:28,700
I just told you.

336
00:15:28,770 --> 00:15:31,170
Have you suffered a recent blow to the head?

337
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:34,100
Hey! You don't have to be so mean!

338
00:15:34,170 --> 00:15:35,600
I'm sorry.

339
00:15:35,670 --> 00:15:37,800
Have you suffered a recent blow to the head?

340
00:15:40,670 --> 00:15:43,170
- No, you just suck at teaching.  - Really?

341
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,870
Of those two explanations, which one seems the most likely?

342
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:47,700
Oh, God...

343
00:15:47,770 --> 00:15:49,770
Sheldon, I'm trying to understand,

344
00:15:49,840 --> 00:15:51,170
but you're going too fast.

345
00:15:51,240 --> 00:15:53,270
Can you just back up a little bit?

346
00:15:53,340 --> 00:15:55,640
All right.

347
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:59,100
It's a warm summer evening in ancient Greece...

348
00:15:59,170 --> 00:16:00,770
Not that far back!

349
00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:02,370
Okay!

350
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:04,540
At what point did you begin to feel lost?

351
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:05,700
I don't know.

352
00:16:05,770 --> 00:16:07,670
Where were we looking up at the night sky?

353
00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:09,940
- Greece. - Damn it!

354
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,140
There's no need to get frustrated.

355
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:13,900
People learn at different rates.

356
00:16:13,970 --> 00:16:18,770
Unlike objects falling in a vacuum, which...?

357
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:24,940
Squared?

358
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:26,340
No.

359
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:27,800
Aristotle?

360
00:16:27,870 --> 00:16:29,300
No.

361
00:16:29,370 --> 00:16:30,370
Five?

362
00:16:32,300 --> 00:16:35,000
Then I don't know.

363
00:16:35,070 --> 00:16:37,340
Why are you crying?

364
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:39,840
Because I'm stupid!

365
00:16:39,900 --> 00:16:42,100
That's no reason to cry.

366
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:45,770
One cries because one is sad.

367
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:48,140
For example, I cry because others are stupid

368
00:16:48,200 --> 00:16:50,170
and it makes me sad.

369
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:54,640
Okay, can we just please forget about all this extra stuff

370
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:57,500
and can you just tell me what Leonard does?

371
00:16:57,570 --> 00:16:59,100
All right.

372
00:16:59,170 --> 00:17:02,740
Leonard is attempting to learn why sub-atomic particles

373
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:04,200
move the way they do.

374
00:17:04,270 --> 00:17:06,940
Really? That's it?

375
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:08,800
Well, that doesn't sound so complicated.

376
00:17:08,870 --> 00:17:10,070
It's not.

377
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:12,240
That's why Leonard does it.

378
00:17:13,740 --> 00:17:16,840
Okay, I just have one question.

379
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:19,600
What exactly are sub-atomic particles?

380
00:17:19,670 --> 00:17:21,540
A good question.

381
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:22,640
Thank you.

382
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:24,440
And to answer it, we first must ask ourselves:

383
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:25,840
"What is physics?"

384
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:27,100
Oh, balls.

385
00:17:27,170 --> 00:17:30,600
It's a warm summer evening in ancient Greece...

386
00:17:31,970 --> 00:17:34,570
Okay, I got a bone to pick with you.

387
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:36,040
What did I do now?

388
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:39,700
I was in bed with Bernadette, and you text-blocked me.

389
00:17:39,770 --> 00:17:41,800
What?!

390
00:17:41,870 --> 00:17:44,570
We were completely naked, about to devour each other when,

391
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:49,140
you text her that I have a problem with her hanging out with you.

392
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:52,440
You do have a problem with her hanging out with me.

393
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:54,400
Yeah, but that's not what you tell her.

394
00:17:54,470 --> 00:17:56,000
What was I supposed to tell her?

395
00:17:56,070 --> 00:17:58,100
I don't know. Something that doesn't make me come off

396
00:17:58,170 --> 00:18:00,570
as a petty, jealous douche.

397
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:03,800
And what would that be?

398
00:18:05,700 --> 00:18:08,970
Come on, do I have to think of everything?

399
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:10,040
Hey, Leonard.

400
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,900
Am I too late to see the experiment?

401
00:18:14,470 --> 00:18:15,640
What are you doing here?

402
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:17,600
Same thing you're doing here.

403
00:18:17,670 --> 00:18:19,640
I came to see Leonard's experiment.

404
00:18:19,700 --> 00:18:20,970
No, you didn't.

405
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:22,700
You said Leonard's experiment was stupid.

406
00:18:26,670 --> 00:18:29,100
You told her my experiment was stupid?

407
00:18:29,170 --> 00:18:31,470
I was just repeating what Sheldon said.

408
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:34,340
Let's not get off topic.

409
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,770
Bernadette, I need to apologize.

410
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:40,440
I was wrong to tell you who you should be friends with.

411
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:42,800
Should I, um, leave you two alone?

412
00:18:42,870 --> 00:18:44,300
No, Leonard, you should hear this.

413
00:18:44,370 --> 00:18:47,270
Okay, good, 'cause I wasn't really gonna go.

414
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:53,600
Look, I know I come off as confident and worldly, but...

415
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:56,840
the truth is I'm not.

416
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:58,840
We're shocked.

417
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:04,040
Which is why I tend to feel threatened by other guys.

418
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:07,340
Or loud noises, clowns and nuns.

419
00:19:09,540 --> 00:19:12,540
But I now realize how foolish that is.

420
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:13,970
He had a panic attack once

421
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:16,200
when he got his head stuck in a sweater.

422
00:19:17,370 --> 00:19:19,470
It was a full turtleneck.

423
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:21,570
Why aren't you helping me?

424
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:23,200
I don't know.

425
00:19:23,270 --> 00:19:26,200
Maybe because I'm... crazy?!

426
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:33,500
Bernadette, please, I'm asking you to give me another chance.

427
00:19:33,570 --> 00:19:35,170
What do you think, Leonard?

428
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:37,240
Should I give him another chance?

429
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:38,400
It's up to you.

430
00:19:38,470 --> 00:19:40,240
He didn't call your experiment stupid.

431
00:19:42,240 --> 00:19:43,600
Come here, tushy face.

432
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:46,170
"Tushy face."

433
00:19:46,170 --> 00:19:48,970
That is going on Twitter right now.

434
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:57,900
Raj, you should've seen Leonard's experiment.

435
00:19:57,970 --> 00:19:59,500
The interference pattern was so cool

436
00:19:59,570 --> 00:20:01,140
when the electron beam was on.

437
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,570
Glad you enjoyed it.

438
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:04,600
Most people aren't that interested in what I do.

439
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:13,940
Actually, that's not true, Leonard.

440
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:15,240
In fact, recently I've been thinking that

441
00:20:15,300 --> 00:20:17,500
given the parameters of your experiment,

442
00:20:17,570 --> 00:20:19,400
the transport of electrons through the aperture

443
00:20:19,470 --> 00:20:21,670
of the nano-fabricated metal rings is qualitatively no different

444
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:25,770
than the experiment already conducted in the Netherlands.

445
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:29,400
Their observed phase shift

446
00:20:29,470 --> 00:20:31,440
in the diffusing electrons inside the metal ring

447
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:34,040
already conclusively demonstrated the electric analogue

448
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:36,640
of the Aharonov-Bohm quantum-interference effect.

449
00:20:41,540 --> 00:20:43,900
That's it. That's all I know.

450
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:47,340
Oh, wait...!

451
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:50,100
Fig Newtons were named after a town in Massachusetts,

452
00:20:50,170 --> 00:20:51,470
not the scientist.

